WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.070
Disney and Marvel's surprise re-release of
Avengers: Endgame is upon us, and it looks

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:09.070
as though the companies have a single goal
in mind: rake in enough money to make the

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.519
superhero ensemble the highest-grossing movie
of all time.

00:00:12.519 --> 00:00:16.810
There's just one big, blue problem standing
between Endgame and that highly sought-after

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:18.599
top spot: Avatar.

00:00:18.599 --> 00:00:22.800
"The Navi say that every person is born twice.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.199
The second time is when you earn your place
among the people."

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:34.180
Directed by James Cameron, Avatar earned a
whopping $2.749 billion worldwide when it

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.860
launched in 2009.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.760
The flick, a sci-fi epic starring Sam Worthington
as a former marine named Jake who travels

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:45.590
to Pandora and befriends the Na'vi people
went on to score even more money at the box

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:49.750
office when 20th Century Fox decided to re-release
it in 2010.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:54.980
In its second theatrical outing, Avatar surpassed
Cameron's disaster romance epic Titanic to

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.040
become the biggest film in history based on
global revenue.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.750
Avatar now stands tall atop a pile of cash
that amounts to $2.788 billion.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:08.909
Avengers: Endgame, the culmination of over
10 years of the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:13.680
brought to life by sibling filmmakers Joe
and Anthony Russo, quickly soared past the

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.270
$2 billion benchmark at the box office after
its debut.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:23.229
During its initial theatrical release that
officially began on April 26, Endgame effortlessly

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:29.840
beat out Titanic, which took home $2.187 billion
worldwide, to become the second best-grossing

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.790
movie in cinematic history.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.439
"Are you serious?"

00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:35.439
"Yeah."

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:36.439
"Yes!

00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:37.439
Haha!"

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:40.740
Though Endgame's commercial success slowly
tapered off following its massive opening,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:46.780
the film currently posts a staggering $2.751
billion in global revenue, with screenings

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.640
still happening as of Monday, June 24th.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.790
Clearly, Avatar and Avengers: Endgame are
neck and neck.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:57.689
The latter needs roughly $37 million more
in global profits to push past the former

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:01.049
and be crowned as the highest-earning movie
ever.

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:02.350
But is that possible?

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.630
Can Endgame really make nearly $40 million
worldwide when Disney and Marvel kick off

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.590
the re-release on Friday, June 28th?

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:13.470
"If we do this, how do we know it's gonna
end any differently than it did before?"

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:16.340
It seems tough, but not impossible.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.970
When Fox put Avatar back in theaters a year
after its original release, showing off nine

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:26.180
minutes of never-before-seen footage that
cost more than $1 million per minute to produce,

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:29.500
the film took home just over $33 million globally.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:35.070
Accounting for the rise in ticket prices,
from an average $7.89 per ticket in 2010 to

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:40.890
an average $9.01 per ticket in 2019 according
to Box Office Mojo, the Avatar re-release

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:45.560
would have made about $37.7 million had it
been rolled out this year.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:50.250
There's arguably a much bigger market for
Avengers: Endgame than there was for Avatar,

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.350
given the film's mass appeal and diehard fanbase.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:58.080
That will certainly factor into how much money
the Endgame re-release pulls in, as its second

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.280
round of success is largely down to how many
people head out to theaters to see the new

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.780
version of the movie.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.870
The extra content included in the re-release
of Avengers: Endgame should draw viewers back

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:13.590
in, even those who have already watched the
super-sized superhero movie several times.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:17.980
Marvel Studios president Kevin Feige clarified
in an interview with Screen Rant when news

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:22.220
of the re-release went public that the new
version of the movie isn't an alternate or

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:27.170
extended cut; instead, it features extra footage
that's tacked onto the end of the three-hour

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:29.920
film fans have likely already seen before.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:31.540
Feige told Screen Rant:

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:36.040
"[It's] not an extended cut, but there will
be a version going into theaters with a bit

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.350
of a marketing push with a few new things
at the end of the movie.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:44.540
If you stay and watch the movie, after the
credits, there'll be a deleted scene, a little

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.070
tribute, and a few surprises."

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:52.470
"Trust me, true believer…Trust me — "

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:58.130
Yup, it's assumed that the tribute Feige spoke
of is one dedicated to Marvel Comics maestro

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:02.580
Stan Lee, who passed away at the age of 95
in November 2018.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.470
The mystery surrounding the deleted scene
and the "surprises" that await fans after

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:12.020
the credits roll still remains, and Marvel
is probably banking on that intrigue translating

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:13.420
into ticket sales.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:18.480
Normally, curiosity kills the cat, but in
this case, it could be the key to Avengers:

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.760
Endgame shattering the biggest movie record
there is.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.150
The Avengers: Endgame re-release is said to
begin on Friday, June 28th.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:29.500
It's unclear how long the new version will
stay in theaters.

